    "abstract": "Combining topological insulators with chiral magnetic multilayers offers the opportunity to greatly enhance the spin-orbit torque (SOT) driven manipulation of hosted spin textures, however this also exposes devices to the often poor temperature stability of the commonly used, Bi2X3-based (X is a chalcogenic element), topological insulator films. In this work we demonstrate through in-situ -annealing and polarised neutron reflectometry that the multilayer at a Bi2Se3 interface remains magnetically robust up to a temperature of 350◦ C: even as the Bi2Se3 epilayer structurally deteriorates. Through x-ray photoemission electron microscopy, an intermediate annealing temperature of ∼ 200◦ C was found to improve the domain ordering of the multilayer, allowing for field-induced skyrmion nucleation, all whilst sustaining minimal damage to the topological insulator.",
    "keywords": [
      "Topological Insulators",
      "Magnetic Multilayers",
      "Polarised Neutron Reflectometry",
      "Photoemission Electron Microscopy"
    ],
